{#- The API document (Launch A, A1-3).

    Every number and every address on this page is passed in. The one thing
    that must never be written here by hand is a field list: `fields` is walked
    from the model, so a schema change that this page does not describe is a
    failing test rather than a stale paragraph.

    The licence section is the reason this page exists at all. `content/` has
    been public JSON for months; what was missing was somebody saying what a
    consumer is allowed to do with it, and being honest about the parts where
    we do not know. -#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Urban Currents — API</title>
{{ head_extras }}
<style>
{{ css }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main class="uc-page">
  <nav class="uc-nav">
    {%- for link in nav %}
    <a{% if link.cls %} class="{{ link.cls }}"{% endif %} href="{{ link.href }}">{{ link.label }}</a>
    {%- endfor %}
    <span class="uc-nav__lang">EN</span>
  </nav>

  <header class="uc-archive__masthead">
    <h1 class="uc-archive__h1">API</h1>
    <p class="uc-archive__summary">
      Every issue as JSON, at addresses that will not move. Nothing here is
      generated for the API — it is what the pipeline already wrote, in another
      shape. {{ stats.days }} days are available, the oldest from
      {{ stats.first_month }}.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="uc-doc">
    <h2 class="uc-doc__h2">Addresses</h2>
    <dl class="uc-legend__list">
      {%- for ep in endpoints %}
      <dt><code>{{ ep.path }}</code></dt>
      <dd>{{ ep.what }}</dd>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>
    <p class="uc-doc__note">
      There is no rate limit and no key. It is a static file on a CDN; fetch it
      like any other file. {{ cors_note }}
    </p>

    <h2 class="uc-doc__h2">Fields</h2>
    <p class="uc-doc__note">
      Two of these have had a name that disagreed with their meaning, so both
      are spelled out rather than left to be guessed.
    </p>
    <dl class="uc-legend__list">
      {%- for f in fields %}
      <dt><code>{{ f.name }}</code></dt>
      <dd>{{ f.what }}</dd>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>

    <h2 class="uc-doc__h2">What we promise</h2>
    <dl class="uc-legend__list">
      <dt>Addresses</dt>
      <dd>
        <code>api/index.json</code>, <code>api/latest.json</code> and
        <code>api/issues/&lt;date&gt;.json</code> stay where they are. A date
        that has been published keeps its file.
      </dd>
      <dt>Fields</dt>
      <dd>
        Fields are added, not removed or repurposed. If a field's meaning has to
        change, it gets a new name and the old one is left as it was —
        renaming in place is how a consumer ends up silently wrong.
      </dd>
      <dt>Values</dt>
      <dd>
        <code>work_key</code> never changes once assigned. Fields that come from
        OpenAlex keep their OpenAlex names.
      </dd>
      <dt>Breaking it</dt>
      <dd>
        <code>schema_version</code> is <code>{{ schema_version }}</code> and is
        on every document. A change that cannot be made by adding will raise it,
        and the previous version stays reachable for at least ninety days. There
        is no mailing list to announce it on, so the version field is the
        announcement — read it.
      </dd>
      <dt>What is not promised</dt>
      <dd>
        Uptime, and the contents of any particular day. The pipeline runs
        unattended and some days it publishes nothing; that is a
        <code>state</code>, not an outage.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 class="uc-doc__h2">Licence and provenance</h2>
    <p class="uc-doc__note">
      <strong>Three different things are in these files and they are not
      under one licence.</strong> Saying so is not lawyerly caution — it is the
      difference between a source you can build on and one that will surprise
      you later.
    </p>
    <dl class="uc-legend__list">
      <dt>Ours — <strong>CC BY 4.0</strong></dt>
      <dd>
        The selection (which papers on which day), the scores, the badges and
        topic assignments, the day's state, and the headline line. This is the
        work: the deciding, not the describing. Attribute it to Urban Currents,
        Institute for Latent Publics, with a link to the day you used.
      </dd>
      <dt>Not ours — <strong>the sources' terms</strong></dt>
      <dd>
        Titles, identifiers, landing-page URLs, journal names and dates come
        from arXiv and OpenAlex and are passed through unchanged. OpenAlex
        places its data in the public domain (CC0); arXiv metadata is used under
        arXiv's own terms. We do not relicense either.
      </dd>
      <dt>Uncertain — <strong>no open licence offered</strong></dt>
      <dd>
        <code>summary.what</code> and <code>summary.why</code>. A model wrote
        them from an abstract we do not own. Whether that makes them ours to
        license is a question we have not answered, and
        <strong>we will not stamp an open licence on something we have not
        established we can grant.</strong> Read them, quote them with
        attribution as you would any other page on the web; do not assume a
        redistribution right. If that is not enough for your use, the fields
        above it are, and they are the ones under CC BY.
      </dd>
      <dt>Absent on purpose</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Abstracts</strong> are not served: third-party expressive text,
        and an API is a redistribution. <strong>Authors, ORCIDs and
        affiliations</strong> are not served either — a per-day machine-readable
        list of people is a different artefact from a citation and not one we
        cleared. Every item carries a DOI or an arXiv identifier, so the
        authoritative record is one fetch away.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    {# arXiv's terms require this wording exactly, including the disclaimer. #}
    <p class="uc-attribution">
      Thank you to arXiv for use of its open access interoperability. This
      service was not reviewed or approved by, nor does it necessarily express
      or reflect the policies or opinions of, arXiv.
    </p>

    <h2 class="uc-doc__h2">Citing a day</h2>
    <p class="uc-doc__note">
      A day is the citable unit — there is no issue number, and inventing one
      here would make this page the source of a fact that exists nowhere else.
    </p>
    <pre class="uc-doc__cite">Urban Currents ({{ stats.first_month.split(' ')[-1] }}–).
Institute for Latent Publics.
Issue {{ example.date }}. {{ example.url }}
Accessed &lt;date&gt;.</pre>
    <p class="uc-doc__note">
      If you are citing what we selected or scored, cite the day. If you are
      citing a paper we listed, cite the paper — its identifier is in the
      record, and it is not ours to stand in front of.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="uc-footer">
    <p class="uc-footer__ilp">Institute for Latent Publics · free, no ads, no metrics</p>
    <p class="uc-footer__build">urban-currents {{ pipeline_version }}</p>
  </footer>
</main>
</body>
</html>
